  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is performing well against structural expectations\u2014MTD is only 1.1% below DOW-adjusted target, and the 49% YTD revenue growth is real and substantial. Yesterday's 11% daily dip was a capacity issue, not a performance one: only 6 groomers produced $436/groomer against a trailing 30-day baseline of $512, and add-on attach fell from 63% to 58%, signaling some operational softness in upsell discipline when the floor wasn't full. The good news is appointment velocity is strong (up 10% WTD, up 10% MTD), retail is up 9%, and visit-cadence drift is shrinking\u2014customers are coming back more frequently. You should be proud of this location's trajectory.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday's 55% revenue collapse looks dramatic but is entirely capacity-driven: only 2 groomers on the floor versus the typical 3\u20134. However, the underlying pattern is troubling. WTD is down 34%, and the 12+ week visit-gap bucket grew 2.4 percentage points\u2014that's stealth churn, not a weekly fluctuation. MTD the store is actually tracking +13% ahead of DOW-adjusted expectation, so the month-level performance is real, but the weakening cadence and thin groomer coverage (2.2/day MTD vs Port's 4.6/day) suggest you're losing customer stickiness faster than you're onboarding. The lapse pool of 84 customers represents $61K in annual value that needs reactivation focus.\n\nOperationally, both locations need inventory attention on treat tables and bully sticks (negative stock at Hicksville), and Hicksville has zero bather hours scheduled for the next week\u2014clarify whether that's a staffing constraint or strategic choice, because it's limiting throughput.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 7 groomers and 7.0 bather hours today, Hicksville has 3 groomers and 0 bather hours; next 7 days Port scheduled for 34 groomer-days and Hicksville for 13.**"
